    "Tilt-table testing is recommended to distinguish convulsive syncope from epilepsy in certain patients, especially when convulsive movements and marked postictal confusion are present. Approximately 50% of patients with questionable or drug-refractory epilepsy may have positive tilt-table tests suggestive of a vasovagal etiology. Psychogenic pseudosyncope should be suspected in patients with frequent symptoms mimicking VVS. Tilt-table testing can help to differentiate syncope from true seizures. It's not recommended for predicting responses to medical treatments for VVS. Neurogenic OH-related syncope is common in patients with autonomic nervous system issues and requires effective management and evaluation.": {},
